    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, but I need more granular guidance on the coding hierarchy itself. When we're looking at these granular or uneven textural changes—for example, if a lesion shows a sudden, irregular, almost scaly or \"craggy\" texture that isn't standard for a typical mole—how do we navigate the hierarchy between general skin morphology codes and more specific diagnostic codes? Is there a preference for coding the morphology itself versus the suspected underlying pathology when the presentation is so irregular?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly, this is a complex aspect. The ICD-10-CM coding structure prioritizes specificity and accounts for the presentation, associated conditions, and suspected etiology when available. For granular or irregular textural changes, you should first detail the morphology, using a specific skin lesion code that closely describes the unusual appearance—like malignant melanoma or atypical melanocytic proliferation. If there is still uncertainty or if additional information is available, options should be added, focusing on adding the most specific code first. Here, consulting further supports or specialist literature may guide the determination. Hierarchy: Detailed morphology -> Suspected underlying pathology -> Additional conditions or supports. Be meticulous in documenting the descriptions and pathological features, and don’t hesitate to revise codes if newer evidence appears throughout the diagnostic process. Always prioritize specificity, but when in doubt, provide the most informative and comprehensive picture of the patient's condition. If there are still issues with specificity, consider reaching out to the ICD-10 coding specialists or your local coding authority for further assistance.",
      "source": "target",
